==========================================
NETWORKING DAY 13 NOTES — Wireshark Part 2
Date: 12-06-2026
==========================================

WHAT I LEARNED TODAY:
- Follow TCP Stream rebuilds a whole conversation as readable text (red = client to server, blue = server to client)
- Wireshark applies tcp.stream eq N automatically; changing N walks through each conversation
- Plain HTTP is fully readable, and files can be rebuilt from a capture with File -> Export Objects -> HTTP
- HTTP display filters: http.request.method, http.response.code, http.user_agent, http.host
- DNS display filters: dns, dns.qry.name, dns.flags.rcode (3 = NXDOMAIN)
- A DNS response shows its Queries, Answers (with TTL) and [Time: ...] in the details pane
- Statistics -> Conversations and Protocol Hierarchy summarize a whole capture in two clicks
- tshark is the command-line version of Wireshark: -r reads a file, -Y filters, -q -z prints statistics

COMMANDS / ACTIONS I PRACTICED:
[ ] Follow -> TCP Stream on a GET request
[ ] tcp.stream eq 0 / 1 / 2
[ ] http.request.method == "GET", http.response.code == 200
[ ] File -> Export Objects -> HTTP, saved exported_page.html
[ ] dig google.com / MX / a non-existent name, then filtered with dns.*
[ ] Statistics -> Conversations (TCP tab) and Protocol Hierarchy
[ ] (bonus) tshark -r basic_capture.pcapng -Y "http.request"

